_notice() {
    cat <<'EOF'

validity-fprintd is installed but not yet active.

It replaces fprintd: both claim the net.reactivated.Fprint D-Bus name and
cannot run at the same time. Masking fprintd also stops D-Bus activating it
behind your back.

  sudo systemctl mask --now fprintd.service
  sudo systemctl enable --now validity-fprintd.service

Check that your sensor is recognised and provisioned:

  sudo systemctl stop validity-fprintd
  sudo validity-provision
  sudo systemctl start validity-fprintd

If it reports unformatted flash or an unpaired sensor, see "Provisioning a
bare sensor" in /usr/share/doc/validity-fprintd-git/README.md. A sensor that
has been used with Windows Hello is already provisioned and needs none of it.

To configure authentication:

  omarchy setup security fingerprint          # on Omarchy

Elsewhere, add "auth sufficient pam_fprintd.so" to the relevant files in
/etc/pam.d/. Keep it "sufficient" so a failed or unavailable finger falls
through to your password.

EOF
}

post_install() {
    _notice
}

post_upgrade() {
    if systemctl is-active --quiet validity-fprintd.service; then
        systemctl restart validity-fprintd.service || true
    fi
}

pre_remove() {
    systemctl disable --now validity-fprintd.service 2>/dev/null || true
}

post_remove() {
    cat <<'EOF'

validity-fprintd removed. If you masked fprintd for it, restore it with:

  sudo systemctl unmask fprintd.service

Fingerprint PAM rules are left untouched. If nothing else provides
pam_fprintd, remove them so authentication does not point at a missing
service (on Omarchy: omarchy-remove-security-fingerprint).

Sensor state - pairing, enrolments, calibration - lives in the sensor itself
and is unaffected. The host-side calibration cache is /var/lib/validity-fprintd.

EOF
}
